---
title: Adding a new specimen record
sidebar:
  label: Adding records
  order: 2
---

{% steps %}

1. Open `Specimens` from the bottom navigation bar, or from the navigation rail
   on a wide screen.

2. Select the add icon in the app bar to create a specimen. On an empty list,
   select `Create specimen`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    If every specimen gets the same parts, open a specimen that already has
    them and choose `Duplicate part` from the three-dot menu. NAHPU immediately
    creates a new specimen with a copy of each part's type, count, treatments,
    storage, and museum fields. Tissue IDs, barcodes, preparators, dates, time
    taken, PMI, and remarks are not copied; fill them in on the new specimen.
    Nothing is created if the specimen has no parts.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select `Cataloger`.

    This is the person responsible for cataloging the specimen. Choosing a
    cataloger also sets `Preparator` and `Determiner` to the same person.
    Depending on the project's `Field ID` mode, the field number comes from the
    cataloger (in `Personnel ID` mode) or from the project (in `Project ID`
    mode).

4. Change `Preparator` if someone else prepared the voucher or part.

5. Select `Condition`.

    Default values are `Freshly euthanized`, `Good`, `Fair`, `Poor`, `Rotten`,
    `Released`, and `Unknown`. Manage this controlled vocabulary in `Settings`
    > `Specimens` > `Conditions`.

6. Add `Collection date`, `Collection time`, `Prep. date`, and `Prep. time`
   when available.

7. Under `Identification`, select `Taxon`.

    Start typing to search the project's taxon registry, then select the
    matching record. NAHPU fills the `Taxon Info` card from it. Because the
    registry stores records at any rank, specimens not yet identified to
    species can be linked to a family or genus and refined later.

8. Change `Determiner` if someone other than the cataloger identified the
   specimen.

9. Select `ID Confidence` (`Low`, `Medium`, or `High`). This tells reviewers
   how certain the identification is and helps curators prioritize checks. Once
   set, `Identification Method` appears.

{% /steps %}
